"A diplomatic errand took him to Paris in 1672, and the four years he spent there made him a mathematician. He arrived knowing little of the subject. Under the guidance of the Dutch physicist Christiaan Huygens he mastered it, and in the autumn of 1675 he worked out the differential and integral calculus, with the notation, dx and the long S of the integral sign, that is still used. Newton had reached the same results a decade earlier by another route and had published nothing. On a visit to London in 1673 Leibniz showed the Royal Society a wooden model of a machine that could multiply and divide by means of a stepped drum, and was elected a fellow.": "671903d4f3bb",
"A second objection is aimed at the principle itself. If everything has a sufficient reason, then everything that happens could not have been otherwise, including each human choice and Gods choice of this world, and freedom disappears. Leibniz replied that reasons incline without necessitating. His contemporaries were not persuaded, and the charge that his system is Spinozas necessity in more pious language has followed it ever since.": "c10531388999",
"His curiosity about China was unusual for his time and was free of condescension. In 1697 he published a collection of reports from the Jesuit mission, in the preface of which he proposes that China should send missionaries to Europe to teach the practice of ethics. In 1701 the Jesuit Joachim Bouvet sent him from Beijing a diagram of the sixty-four hexagrams of the Book of Changes. Leibniz had recently devised a way of writing every number with only the signs 0 and 1, and he saw at once that if a broken line is read as 0 and an unbroken line as 1, the hexagrams in that arrangement count from 0 to 63. He concluded that the ancient Chinese had possessed his binary arithmetic and later forgotten it. He was wrong about the history. The notation is the one on which every computer now runs.": "0926ac88ab31",
"His last years were poisoned by the dispute over the calculus. Newtons followers accused him of having stolen the idea during his London visits, and in 1712 a committee of the Royal Society, whose report Newton himself secretly drafted, found against him. Historians now agree that the two discoveries were independent. In 1714 his employer became King George I of Great Britain and moved the court to London. Leibniz asked to follow and was told to stay in Hanover and finish the family history, which had reached the year 1005. He died there in November 1716. A friend recorded that he was buried more like a robber than like what he was, the ornament of his country. Of the whole court, only his secretary attended the funeral.": "a774322e3dda",
"Leibniz published one philosophical book in his lifetime, the Theodicy, a long and courtly defense of the goodness of God. The works on which his reputation now rests were not written for print. The Discourse on Metaphysics of 1686 was a private summary. The Monadology was composed in 1714 for a few correspondents and appeared only after his death, and its title was supplied by an editor. His reply to Locke, the New Essays, he withheld because Locke had died, and it was printed in 1765. The rest is in some fifteen thousand letters and a mass of notes. As a result there were for two centuries several Leibnizes. The eighteenth century knew the optimist of the Theodicy. Bertrand Russell, reading the logical papers around 1900, argued that the public system was a façade and that the real philosophy was a logic kept in the desk. The complete edition of his writings, begun in 1923, is expected to take many more decades.": "1b9c3f60d4bb",
"Leibniz said that all our reasoning rests on two principles. The first is that a contradiction cannot be true. The second, which he made his own, is the principle of sufficient reason: there can be no fact real or existing, no statement true, unless there be a sufficient reason, why it should be so and not otherwise, although these reasons usually cannot be known by us. Nothing simply happens to be the case. From this one demand he drew a whole account of the world. If there must be a reason why this universe exists and no other, the reason can only be that it is the best of those that were possible, and that a perfect being chose it for that reason. If two things were exactly alike in every respect, there could be no reason for one to be here and the other there, so no two things are exactly alike.": "a43a4ace83e6",
"Leibniz was born in Leipzig in 1646, two years before the end of the Thirty Years War, which had killed perhaps a third of the population of the German lands. His father, a professor of moral philosophy, died when he was six and left a library. The boy was given the run of it at eight, taught himself Latin from an illustrated history, and by his teens had read the ancient authors and the medieval logicians. He took a doctorate in law at twenty and was offered a professorship, which he refused. He wanted, he said, a larger sphere of action, and he entered the service of a prince.": "99d0e421608e",
"No post could be found for him in Paris, and in 1676 he accepted one at the small court of Hanover, where he remained for the rest of his life. His duties were to keep the library and to write the history of the ruling family. Around them he pursued a range of projects that has never been matched. He designed windmills to drain the silver mines of the Harz mountains. He worked for the reunion of the Catholic and Protestant churches. He founded the academy of sciences in Berlin, and he studied fossils, Chinese philosophy, and the origins of languages. He corresponded with more than a thousand people. Very little of his philosophy was printed. It lay in letters and in drafts, and scholars are still editing them.": "dcaea4ab2cdf",
"On November 1, 1755, an earthquake destroyed Lisbon while the churches were full for All Saints Day, and tens of thousands died. Four years later Voltaire published Candide, in which the tutor Pangloss, a disciple of Leibniz, passes through war, shipwreck, the earthquake, and the Inquisition explaining at each disaster that all is for the best. The satire is unfair in one respect. Leibniz did not say that everything in the world is good. He said that a world with free creatures and regular laws could not have had less evil in total without being worse in some other way, and that we, who see a fragment, are in no position to judge the whole. But the unfairness does not remove the difficulty. A claim that would be equally consistent with any amount of suffering explains none of it, and it is hard to say to the parents of a drowned child that the alternative worlds were worse.": "dce78b9fb298",
"One image in Leibniz has a famous Buddhist twin. Each monad, he says, is a living mirror that reflects the entire universe from its own point of view. The Huayan school of Chinese Buddhism describes the net of the god Indra, which stretches without limit in every direction and has a jewel at every knot. Each jewel reflects all the others, and in each reflection all the others are reflected again. The Huayan masters used the net to teach that every phenomenon contains and is contained by every other. The two images arose with no contact between them, and they are put to opposite uses. Leibnizs mirrors are substances: each monad is a self-contained thing that could exist if no other did, and it only represents the rest. A jewel in Indras net is nothing apart from the others. It exists by interdependence, and that is what the Buddhist means by calling it empty.": "f4e6467af9cc",
"The principle of sufficient reason has a nearer relative in dependent origination: when this exists, that comes to be. Both deny that anything arises without conditions. They differ on where the series ends. Leibniz argues that the chain of reasons must terminate in a being that contains the reason for its own existence, and that this is God. The Buddha declined to posit a first cause and described a web of conditions with no beginning that can be found. The problem that occupied Leibniz for forty years, how a good creator could permit evil, therefore does not arise in Buddhism in that form. Suffering is explained by craving and ignorance, with no one having chosen the arrangement (Dharma and the West, D4).": "26ad192158bb",
"The strangest consequence concerns what the world is made of. Anything extended in space can be divided, and what can be divided is a collection and depends on its parts. The ultimate constituents must therefore be simple, without parts, and so without extension. Leibniz called them monads. Since a thing without parts cannot be pushed or altered from outside, the Monads have no windows, through which anything could come in or go out. Each unfolds its own states from within, as a mind does, and what a monad has is perception, in degrees that run from the dim stirrings of what we call matter to the clear awareness of a rational soul. Each perceives the whole universe from its own position and is a perpetual living mirror of the universe. No monad acts on any other. They agree with one another because they were set in order together at the creation, like clocks made so well that they keep the same time forever. He called this the pre-established harmony.": "00281b7db9ff",
